          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Chebyshev polynomials of the second kind",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#8b5cc7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "  Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "U_n(x)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be polynomials with the property that",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p59-q35-definition",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "U_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\frac{\\sin((n+1)\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}.",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p59-q35-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $U_0(x)=1$ and $U_1(x)=2x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$U_0(x)=1$, $U_1(x)=2x$.\n\nUse the definition with $n=0,1$ and $\\sin2\\theta=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$.",
          "worked": "For $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}U_0(\\cos\\theta)&=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}=1,\\\\U_1(\\cos\\theta)&=\\frac{\\sin2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},\\\\U_1(\\cos\\theta)&=\\frac{2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},\\\\U_1(\\cos\\theta)&=2\\cos\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting $x=\\cos\\theta$ gives $U_0(x)=1$ and $U_1(x)=2x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      },
      {
        "id": "p59-q35-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that\n$$\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)=2\\cos\\theta\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)=2\\cos\\theta\\sin((n+1)\\theta)$.\n\nExpand $\\sin(n\\theta+2\\theta)$, use the double-angle identities and factor out $2\\cos\\theta$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)&=\\sin(n\\theta)\\cos2\\theta+\\cos(n\\theta)\\sin2\\theta+\\sin(n\\theta),\\\\\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)&=\\sin(n\\theta)(\\cos2\\theta+1)+\\cos(n\\theta)\\sin2\\theta,\\\\\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)&=2\\sin(n\\theta)\\cos^2\\theta+2\\cos(n\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta,\\\\\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)&=2\\cos\\theta\\bigl(\\sin(n\\theta)\\cos\\theta+\\cos(n\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\bigr),\\\\\\sin((n+2)\\theta)+\\sin(n\\theta)&=2\\cos\\theta\\sin((n+1)\\theta).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p59-q35-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $U_{n+1}(x)=2xU_n(x)-U_{n-1}(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$U_{n+1}(x)=2xU_n(x)-U_{n-1}(x)$.\n\nDivide the identity in part b by $\\sin\\theta$, use the definition and substitute $x=\\cos\\theta$.",
          "worked": "Divide the identity in part b by $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$ and rearrange:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\sin((n+2)\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}&=2\\cos\\theta\\frac{\\sin((n+1)\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}-\\frac{\\sin(n\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta},\\\\U_{n+1}(\\cos\\theta)&=2\\cos\\theta\\,U_n(\\cos\\theta)-U_{n-1}(\\cos\\theta).\\end{align*}$$\nWith $x=\\cos\\theta$,\n$$U_{n+1}(x)=2xU_n(x)-U_{n-1}(x),\\qquad n\\ge1.$$\nThe polynomial identity holds for all $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p59-q35-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find $U_4(x)$, and hence an identity for $\\sin5\\theta$ in terms of powers of $\\sin\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$U_4(x)=16x^4-12x^2+1$.\n\n$\\sin5\\theta=16\\sin^5\\theta-20\\sin^3\\theta+5\\sin\\theta$.\n\nUse the recurrence, then substitute $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}U_2(x)&=2x(2x)-1,\\\\U_2(x)&=4x^2-1,\\\\U_3(x)&=2x(4x^2-1)-2x,\\\\U_3(x)&=8x^3-4x,\\\\U_4(x)&=2x(8x^3-4x)-(4x^2-1),\\\\U_4(x)&=16x^4-12x^2+1.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the definition and $\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin5\\theta&=\\sin\\theta\\,U_4(\\cos\\theta),\\\\\\sin5\\theta&=\\sin\\theta(16\\cos^4\\theta-12\\cos^2\\theta+1),\\\\\\sin5\\theta&=\\sin\\theta\\bigl(16(1-\\sin^2\\theta)^2-12(1-\\sin^2\\theta)+1\\bigr),\\\\\\sin5\\theta&=16\\sin^5\\theta-20\\sin^3\\theta+5\\sin\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $\\sin\\theta=0$, both sides are zero, so the identity holds for every $\\theta$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
  },
